Android port — r-mail

The android app is a mobile frontend for a linux daemon. It
communicates using the same protocol but pulls messages instead
of listening for pushes. Every user still needs a linux daemon
running somewhere (home machine, VPS, etc.) as their always-on
mailbox.

Android-to-Android routing:
  Alice's phone -> Alice's daemon -> Bob's daemon <- Bob's phone
  Standalone android-to-android (no daemon) is not feasible due
  to carrier-grade NAT (CGNAT). NAT hole punching (UDP/STUN) is
  unreliable through CGNAT. Tor hidden services could bypass NAT
  but add a large dependency. For now, a daemon is required.


ARCHITECTURE

  The android app does NOT need a "poll" contact type or any
  special daemon-side config. The existing protocol already
  handles it:

  - Undelivered messages sit in the sender's outbox and retry
    each sync cycle. The daemon already does this.
  - The /poll endpoint lets the android app pull those pending
    messages. Once pulled, they're marked delivered in outbox
    state and the daemon stops retrying.
  - Failed push attempts to android devices don't speed up the
    sync timer (did_work is only set on success).
  - The daemon now uses socket.select() for parallel outgoing
    connections, so multiple unreachable contacts hit one shared
    timeout instead of sequential timeouts.


LINUX DAEMON CHANGES

  New endpoint: POST /poll
    Request:  {"from": "alice", "token": "secret"}
    Response: {
      "messages": [
        {"subject": "hello", "message_id": "uuid", "body": "text"}
      ],
      "deletions": ["msg-id-1", "msg-id-2"]
    }

    The daemon checks outbox state for undelivered messages to
    this contact, returns them, and marks them delivered. Also
    returns any pending deletions.

  Pending deletion queue
    Currently delete notifications are fire-and-forget. For
    contacts that poll, deletions need to be queued in state
    until the next poll picks them up.

    Stored in .state/pending-deletions.json:
      {"alice": ["msg-id-1", "msg-id-2"], "bob": ["msg-id-3"]}

    When the sender deletes an outbox file (or removes a to: line),
    if the push notification to the recipient fails, queue the
    message_id here. The /poll endpoint returns and clears these.

  No "poll" field needed in contacts
    The daemon just tries to push. If push fails, the message
    stays pending. If the contact polls, they get it that way.
    The only cost is wasted connection timeouts on push attempts,
    which are now parallel (socket.select) and bounded at 8s total
    regardless of contact count.


ANDROID APP

  Language: C + HTML5 + WASM
    Core logic (networking, polling, state) written in C,
    compiled to WASM. UI in plain HTML5 (no CSS, no JavaScript).
    Wrapped in a minimal Android WebView shell (~50 lines Kotlin).
    The same WASM build also runs in a desktop browser.

  Core
    - Local inbox/outbox stored in app private storage
    - Contacts/secrets management
    - Background polling via Android WorkManager (survives doze)
    - Adaptive poll timer per contact:
      starts at 5 min, shrinks on new messages, grows on idle
    - All timers reset on app/phone restart

  Sending
    - User writes message in app, picks contact(s)
    - App sends POST /deliver to each recipient's daemon
    - Outgoing connections — no NAT issues
    - Retry queue for failed sends

  Receiving
    - WorkManager job polls each contact's daemon via POST /poll
    - New messages saved to local inbox
    - Deletions applied to local inbox
    - Android notification for new messages

  Deletion
    - User deletes from inbox -> app sends POST /delete to
      sender's daemon (outgoing, no NAT issue)
    - Sender deletes -> next poll returns deletion ID -> app
      removes locally

  Self-hosting web UI
    - The app hosts the HTML5 interface on a local port
    - Accessible at localhost:8025 on the phone
    - If the phone's port is forwarded on wifi router, friends
      can visit the interface from their devices on the LAN
    - Same interface the daemon could serve on linux

  UI
    - Inbox list (sender, subject, preview)
    - Message view (plain text body)
    - Compose screen (to: field, subject/filename, body)
    - Contacts screen (add/edit/remove, host/port/token)
    - Settings (poll intervals, notification preferences)

  Storage
    - SQLite for messages and state
    - Encrypted storage for contacts/tokens


BROWSER UI (shared between android and linux)

  The linux daemon already serves HTTP. It could serve an HTML5
  interface on the same port, replacing the JSON health check on
  GET / with a full web UI (move health check to GET /status).

  This gives linux users a browser-based option:
    - Visit localhost:8025 to read/write/delete messages
    - Same HTML5+WASM code used by the android app
    - No new dependencies — the daemon is already an HTTP server
    - Could be toggled with a config option (WEB_UI = true)

  The web UI talks to the daemon via the existing JSON endpoints
  (/deliver, /delete, /poll). The only new thing is serving
  static files (the HTML and WASM).


PROTOCOL SUMMARY

  Existing (no changes):
    POST /deliver  — send a message
    POST /delete   — notify of deletion
    GET /          — health check (or web UI)

  New:
    POST /poll     — pull pending messages and deletions

  The android app uses /deliver and /delete as a client (outgoing).
  It uses /poll to receive (also outgoing — it initiates).
  It never needs to listen on a port.


OPEN QUESTIONS

  - How to handle the initial sync when the app is first set up?
    Poll with no "since" param could return all undelivered
    messages (i.e. everything pending for this contact).

  - Should the linux daemon auto-detect poll contacts (if someone
    polls you, mark them as poll-capable) or just always queue
    deletions for everyone? Queuing for everyone is simpler and
    avoids the distinction entirely.

  - How much of the C/WASM core can be shared between the android
    app and the linux browser UI? Ideally all of it — the same
    .wasm file runs in both contexts.

  - For the web UI: authentication? Currently the daemon trusts
    localhost. If serving a web UI, it should probably only bind
    to 127.0.0.1 for the UI, or require a local auth token.


NAT RESEARCH NOTES

  Why phones can't receive connections:

  Phones sit behind carrier-grade NAT (CGNAT). The carrier
  assigns the phone a private IP (e.g. 10.x.x.x) and many
  phones share one public IP. There's no router to configure
  port forwarding on — the carrier controls the NAT.

  Approaches considered and rejected:

  1. Phone wifi-to-mobile-data loopback
     Idea: phone sends a packet over wifi to its own cellular IP,
     tricking the cellular NAT into creating an inbound mapping.
     Why it fails:
     - The OS kernel routes traffic to its own IP via loopback
       internally. The packet never leaves the phone, never
       touches the cellular NAT, no mapping is created.
     - Even if forced out wifi, the cellular IP is private
       (behind CGNAT). The wifi network has no route to it.
     - NAT mappings are destination-specific. A mapping created
       by sending to 1.2.3.4:9000 only allows replies from
       1.2.3.4:9000, not arbitrary inbound connections.

  2. Spoofing local IP / MAC address
     Idea: phone spoofs a LAN IP or MAC to trick the carrier
     NAT into thinking it's local traffic.
     Why it fails:
     - Cellular networks don't use Ethernet. Device identity is
       at the radio/SIM/IMEI level, not MAC.
     - Even on wifi, spoofing a local IP just impersonates
       another LAN device. Doesn't create inbound NAT mappings
       from the internet.

  3. UDP hole punching (how games/WebRTC do it)
     Both sides send packets to each other simultaneously,
     creating NAT mappings that let the other's packet through.
     Requires a STUN/signaling server to coordinate timing
     and exchange addresses. Unreliable through CGNAT / symmetric
     NAT, which is what most carriers use.

  4. Hamachi / Tailscale / ZeroTier approach
     These create a VPN tunnel. Every client connects OUTWARD
     to a central coordination server. The server brokers P2P
     connections (via hole punching) and relays traffic when
     P2P fails. A virtual network adapter makes it look like
     a LAN. This works, but requires a central server.

  What actually works:

  - Central relay server (both sides connect outward to it)
  - IPv6 (no NAT at all, globally routable — but not universal)
  - Tor hidden services (.onion, NAT-transparent, but heavy)
  - Just having a linux daemon with an open port (current design)

  Conclusion:

  The r-mail design already solves this: users share public IPs
  and open ports manually. The android app is a frontend for a
  linux daemon that has an open port. Standalone android-to-android
  without any server is not feasible with current internet
  infrastructure unless both sides have IPv6.
